            <ArticleTitle>Magnetic resonance angiography and flow quantification of coronary arteries.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>339-47</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Although further work is needed to improve spatial resolution and flow contrast as well as to lessen dependence on a regular cardiac rhythm, the potential clinical utility of MR coronary angiography is substantial. For the first time, information regarding coronary artery anatomy can be acquired in a completely noninvasive manner.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
